choices(56,[('Stationary stator as primary, rotating rotor as secondary, with flux crossing an air gap.','This is the source mapping.'),('Rotating rotor as the directly supplied primary and stator as the rotating secondary.','The supplied primary is the stationary stator.'),('Both stator and rotor supplied through separate brushes as two conduction-fed primaries.','This removes the stated induction-fed secondary analogy.'),('The stator supplies flux only through a solid mechanical contact with no magnetic air gap.','The source explicitly identifies an air gap.')])
choices(58,[('Conducting rotor bars joined by end rings.','They form the closed induced-current paths.'),('Rotor bars joined only through external brushes and a commutator.','The source excludes an external rotor electrical connection.'),('Each isolated rotor bar with both ends left open.','Open bars do not form the described complete circuits.'),('Only the stator supply wires, with no conductive closure between rotor bars.','The rotor circuits close through their end rings, not external stator wires.')])
choices(63,[('Pulse count determines nominal displacement; pulse rate determines nominal speed.','Count and rate have these respective motion roles.'),('Pulse rate determines displacement and pulse count determines speed, regardless of elapsed time.','The roles are exchanged.'),('Pulse width alone determines each full-step angle while count sets only winding temperature.','The declared step angle and sequence, not pulse width alone, establish nominal displacement.'),('The number of winding phases equals pulse rate, and rotor-tooth count equals command count.','Physical construction counts differ from command signals.')])
