a([1703,1712],'ac-control-scope','Scope of the AC-control comparison','The AC slide calls speed control difficult, while the summary gives VFD-based PMSM and industrial AC-servo applications. Which interpretation is defensible?', [('Control difficulty is a comparison with an unstated design/era context, not a claim that AC drives cannot provide controlled robotic motion.','The later examples explicitly use controlled AC drives.'),('All AC motors have fixed speed regardless of supply frequency or drive electronics.','The same source discusses frequency-based control.'),('Any AC motor with a VFD automatically has exact position feedback and specified servo accuracy.','A variable-frequency drive alone does not establish a position-servo loop or accuracy rating.'),('Every DC drive is lower-cost and easier to control than every AC drive for all power levels.','The source gives no universal matched-system cost/control proof.')], 'Keep the source comparison qualified and distinguish speed-control electronics from a complete position servo.',loc={1703:'AC cheaper/speed-control-difficult bullet and variable-frequency supply statement.',1712:'PMSM with VFD and brushless AC servo application bullets.'},conflict='Broad AC/DC cost and control statements omit comparison conditions; later controlled AC examples require qualified scope.')
